+      // PHP casts an `expr`, so every alternative of $.expression is a legal
+      // cast operand. Most of them reach this rule already: the ones that
+      // start with the operand itself -- assignment, binary, conditional --
+      // arrive via $._unary_expression and then extend rightwards under
+      // PREC.CAST, so `(int) $x + 1` needs no alternative of its own. What
+      // needs listing is the constructs that lead with a KEYWORD and swallow
+      // to the right. Upstream listed two of them, include and include_once,
+      // and the remaining four are the same shape and were simply missed:
+      // require and require_once (the other half of the same pair), match,
+      // and yield / yield from. Each was checked against php -l.
       cast_expression: $ => prec(PREC.CAST, seq(
         '(',
         field('type', $.cast_type),
